test('FWE-001-A02 POSIX canonical owner paths accept safe absolute and reject unsafe', () => {
  assert.equal(safeOwnerPath('/tmp/frade/owner'), true)
# Direction bootstrap (task 2.3)

Use a reviewed, installed public control release. This command does not treat the unfinished W01 implementation as a deployed release. The trusted installer places `bootstrap-authority.json` in `<Git common>/frade-workflow/`. It binds the canonical primary source root, Git common, approved workspace parent, selected policy version/release, exact bundle paths and SHA256 values, and approved request hashes. The installer is outside the request. `approved: true` or any other request field cannot grant create authority.

The authority shape is:

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "sourceRoot": "/absolute/canonical/frade",
  "gitCommon": "/absolute/canonical/frade/.git",
  "workspaceParent": "/absolute/canonical/owners",
  "policy": {
    "version": "reviewed-release-version",
    "release": "64-lowercase-hex-release-id",
    "sha256": "64-lowercase-hex-policy-hash",
    "artifact": "policy/agent-workflow.md"
  },
  "bundle": [
    { "path": "policy/agent-workflow.md", "sha256": "64-lowercase-hex-policy-hash" }
  ],
  "requestHashes": ["64-lowercase-hex-plan-intent-hash"]
}
```

The reviewed installer must supply the real release ID and all selected bundle files. `create` verifies every bundle file against both the selected committed baseline and the live primary checkout before writing. Fixture authorities in the tests explicitly stand in for this external installer. The CLI has no release installation or publication command in task 2.3.

Create an absolute-path JSON request with these fields:

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "example-direction",
  "title": "Example direction",
  "goal": "Describe the bounded goal",
  "users": ["intended users"],
  "outcomes": ["observable result"],
  "constraints": ["preserve existing behavior"],
  "exclusions": ["unrelated product files"],
  "sourceRoot": "/absolute/canonical/frade",
  "gitCommon": "/absolute/canonical/frade/.git",
  "workspaceParent": "/absolute/canonical/owners",
  "baseline": "40-lowercase-hex-committed-baseline",
  "policy": {
    "version": "reviewed-release-version",
    "release": "64-lowercase-hex-release-id",
    "sha256": "64-lowercase-hex-policy-hash",
    "artifact": "policy/agent-workflow.md"
  },
  "publication": {
    "remote": "git@example.test:team/frade.git",
    "ref": "refs/heads/codex/example-direction",
    "authorization": "pending human decision"
  }
}
```

From the registered primary source root:

```sh
node scripts/directions/cli.mjs plan /absolute/path/request.json
node scripts/directions/cli.mjs create /absolute/path/request.json
node scripts/directions/cli.mjs check /absolute/canonical/owners/example-direction/docs/engineering/directions/example-direction/direction.json
```

`plan` returns a read-only JSON preview with exact branch, worktree, seed paths, committed baseline, policy, publication destination, unknowns, authority path, intent path and an intent hash. A trusted installer records that hash before `create`. `create` returns JSON exit 0 only after the branch is registered and seed files exist; blocked JSON uses exit 2. The branch is `codex/<id>` and the manifest is branch-local. The seed preserves inherited root rules, marks model assignment and downstream gates unresolved, and creates no product package.

The partial recovery protocol writes `<Git common>/frade-workflow/intents/<id>.json` with exclusive creation before `git worktree add`. It records the request hash, branch, target and baseline. The worktree command uses Git argv and command-local `core.autocrlf=false`. The CLI verifies actual Git registration and baseline before seeding. A separate `<id>.complete.json` receipt is written after seed files. A retry with the same request either finishes the retained work or returns the existing registered owner; it never cleans, resets or deletes an uncertain target. A different request, existing foreign branch/target, changed policy, unregistered target or case collision blocks without overwriting it. A partial failure returns `BLOCKED` and retains its intent and any registered worktree for inspection.

`check` validates the v2 manifest, selected policy bytes and registered Git owner. It does not certify accepted requirements, reviews, checks or publication. `status`, `review`, `checkpoint` and `publish` report `NOT_IMPLEMENTED` until their later tasks. Safe research and decision recording can continue while acceptance cases, numeric limits, exact role assignments or publication authorization remain unresolved; dependent implementation and publication stay blocked.
